cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  contactsched: ground-station contact scheduling with the contention and
  fairness cost measured
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-25
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A contention and fairness measurement harness for ground-station contact
  scheduling, not a scheduler to put in front of a real network. It computes
  visibility windows from synthetic two-line element sets through sgp4 and an
  azimuth-dependent horizon mask, schedules the resulting requests two ways on
  the same candidate grid -- a documented priority-ordered first-fit and the
  proven optimum as a clique mixed-integer program on HiGHS through
  scipy.optimize.milp -- and then measures what the schedule cost: per-request
  shortfall in contacts and seconds, Jain's index, the max-min share, and the
  number of priority inversions, pairs in which a more urgent request ended
  worse served than a less urgent one. The exact method agrees with literal
  2**n bitmask enumeration, which shares no part of its formulation, on 140 of
  140 small instances across four objective and fairness configurations with a
  worst objective difference of 0.000e+00 over 6964736 enumerated subsets. The
  product specification named a PuLP backend; pulp 4.1.0 returns an empty list
  from listSolvers(onlyAvailable=True) in the environment these numbers were
  measured in, so it ships no solver and a pulp-backed solve would raise rather
  than return a schedule. pulp is therefore not a dependency and the deviation
  is published as a measurement reproducible with one command. astropy and
  OR-Tools are not installed either, and no number in this repository is a
  cross-check against any of the three; the independent geometric check of the
  elevation is a second derivation written in this package, from the spherical
  law of cosines on geocentric latitude and longitude, agreeing with the
  Cartesian form to 8.086e-12 degrees over 57624 samples. Window boundaries
  agree with a dense one-second scan to 0.5859 s, and no window duration
  exceeds an independently derived closed-form spherical bound, with measured
  ratios between 0.758 and 0.924. The analytic baseline is published as
  measured whether it wins or loses: priority-ordered first-fit ties the proven
  optimum on 46.88 per cent of 160 instances with a mean gap of 0.029962 and a
  tail to 0.176871, and neither of the two heuristic orderings dominates the
  other. The learned conflict predictor, a gradient-boosted classifier over 23
  pre-scheduling features, reaches 0.984596 area under the ROC curve against
  0.922952 for the strongest contention-ratio baseline on 725 held-out requests
  from a disjoint seed range, 0.952907 against 0.838021 on the subset where the
  trivially over-asked requests have been removed, and loses to that baseline
  on calibration with an expected calibration error of 0.027302 against
  0.012237. That loss is published unchanged. The contention ratio the
  specification named, demand over supply per station-window, was measured
  before the learned model was fitted and is the weakest of three available
  baselines, so the strongest was used instead. The measured price of enforcing
  every declared minimum contact count is zero on all sixteen of one hundred
  and sixty instances where the constrained program is feasible at all, which
  contradicts the expectation written before the measurement; a constructed
  instance where the price is one third of the objective is published beside
  it, and one hundred and forty-four of one hundred and sixty instances are
  infeasible under their own floors because a request declares more contacts
  than its own passes can carry. Six defects were found during the build, three
  of them by Hypothesis and by none of the 611 hand-written tests: a
  ZeroDivisionError in Jain's index at the smallest positive double, an azimuth
  of exactly 360.0 outside its documented half-open range, and a horizon mask
  that was not periodic when two breakpoints shared an azimuth. All training
  data is synthetic, generated by a seeded script whose distributional choices
  are printed in the committed validation output, and no flight data,
  operational request log or catalogue element set is used anywhere. Validation
  is level 2. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved
  for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - satellite-communications
  - ground-station
  - contact-scheduling
  - antenna-contention
  - fairness
  - jain-index
  - sgp4
  - mixed-integer-programming
references:
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Hoots
        given-names: "F. R."
      - family-names: Roehrich
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "Models for Propagation of NORAD Element Sets"
    number: "Spacetrack Report No. 3"
    year: 1980
    notes: >-
      The two-line element format implemented in contactsched.tle, including the
      modulo-10 checksum and the implied decimal point in the eccentricity
      field. Nothing in this repository reimplements SGP4 itself.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Vallado
        given-names: "D. A."
      - family-names: Crawford
        given-names: "P."
      - family-names: Hujsak
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Kelso
        given-names: "T. S."
    title: "Revisiting Spacetrack Report #3"
    number: "AIAA 2006-6753"
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      The reference SGP4 implementation that the sgp4 package wraps and that
      every position in this repository comes from.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Vallado
        given-names: "D. A."
    title: "Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications"
    edition: "4th"
    publisher:
      name: "Microcosm Press"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Section 3.2 for geodetic and geocentric latitude, section 3.7 for the
      IAU-1982 GMST polynomial implemented independently in
      contactsched.geometry and measured against sgp4.propagation.gstime to
      2.211e-11 rad, and section 4.4 for the topocentric south-east-zenith
      rotation.
  - type: report
    authors:
      - name: "National Imagery and Mapping Agency"
    title: "Department of Defense World Geodetic System 1984"
    number: "NIMA TR8350.2, third edition, amendment 1"
    year: 2000
    notes: >-
      The ellipsoid. The maximum angle between its normal and the position
      vector, 0.19242430115956036 degrees, is the independently derived bound
      that falsified a hard-coded constant during this build.
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Jain
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Chiu
        given-names: "D.-M."
      - family-names: Hawe
        given-names: "W."
    title: >-
      A Quantitative Measure of Fairness and Discrimination for Resource
      Allocation in Shared Computer Systems
    number: "DEC TR-301"
    year: 1984
    notes: >-
      The fairness index implemented as contactsched.fairness.jain_index. Its
      value of 1.0 for an all-zero allocation is a documented convention, not a
      measurement, and is why the max-min share is always reported beside it.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Bertsekas
        given-names: "D."
      - family-names: Gallager
        given-names: "R."
    title: "Data Networks"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Prentice-Hall"
    year: 1992
    notes: >-
      Section 6.5.2 for max-min fairness as the lexicographic maximisation of
      the smallest share. This package reports the max-min share an allocation
      achieves and claims nothing about its optimality.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Wolsey
        given-names: "L. A."
    title: "Integer Programming"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      Chapter 1 for the clique formulation of interval conflict, which is exact
      rather than a relaxation for interval graphs and is why testing candidate
      start times suffices in contactsched.exact.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Huangfu
        given-names: "Q."
      - family-names: Hall
        given-names: "J. A. J."
    title: "Parallelizing the dual revised simplex method"
    journal: "Mathematical Programming Computation"
    volume: 10
    issue: 1
    year: 2018
    notes: >-
      The HiGHS solver that scipy.optimize.milp dispatches to, and the backend
      every exact result in this repository was produced by.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Ke
        given-names: "G."
    title: >-
      LightGBM: a highly efficient gradient boosting decision tree
    journal: "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"
    volume: 30
    year: 2017
    notes: >-
      The histogram-based boosting algorithm behind
      sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingClassifier, which is the learned
      conflict predictor. scikit-learn is bounded >=1.9,<1.10 in pyproject.toml
      because every measured score is a property of its fitted trees.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Brier
        given-names: "G. W."
    title: >-
      Verification of forecasts expressed in terms of probability
    journal: "Monthly Weather Review"
    volume: 78
    issue: 1
    year: 1950
    notes: >-
      The Brier score, reported alongside a constant forecast at the base rate
      because a Brier score has no meaning without one.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Niculescu-Mizil
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Caruana
        given-names: "R."
    title: "Predicting good probabilities with supervised learning"
    journal: "Proceedings of the 22nd International Conference on Machine Learning"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Why a boosted ensemble's raw scores are over-confident at the extremes,
      which is what the measured reliability table shows and why the
      one-feature logistic baseline wins on calibration.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Strobl
        given-names: "C."
      - family-names: Boulesteix
        given-names: "A.-L."
      - family-names: Zeileis
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Hothorn
        given-names: "T."
    title: >-
      Bias in random forest variable importance measures: illustrations,
      sources and a solution
    journal: "BMC Bioinformatics"
    volume: 8
    year: 2007
    notes: >-
      Why the permutation importances reported for the learned model describe
      that fitted model on that generator and are not evidence about
      ground-station scheduling.
